# Optional. Used for self-referential links in production.
APP_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# Required for real "Sign in with Google" (replaces the old fake local login).
# 1. Go to https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
# 2. Create Credentials -> OAuth client ID -> Application type: Web application
# 3. Authorized JavaScript origins: add http://localhost:3000 for dev, plus your
#    deployed site's URL (e.g. https://your-app.example.com) for production.
# 4. No redirect URI or client secret is needed for this flow (Google Identity
#    Services ID-token sign-in) — just the Client ID below.
# 5. Set the SAME value in both variables: GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID is read by the server
#    to verify tokens, VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID is baked into the client bundle so
#    the Sign in with Google button can initialize.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=""
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=""

# Required. Signs the app's own session cookie after a user is verified (by Google or
# by email OTP).
# Generate one with: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
SESSION_SECRET=""

# Optional — for the "Create Account with email" flow's one-time verification code.
# Without these set, the server logs the code to its own console instead of emailing it
# (fine for local testing, not for real students — set these before deploying).
# Any SMTP provider works. Quick options:
#  - Gmail: host smtp.gmail.com, port 587, user = your Gmail address, pass = a 16-char
#    "App Password" from https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords (NOT your normal
#    Gmail password — that won't work and Google will reject plain password SMTP login).
#  - A transactional email service (Resend, SendGrid, Mailgun, etc.) — use the SMTP
#    credentials from their dashboard the same way.
SMTP_HOST=""
SMTP_PORT="587"
SMTP_USER=""
SMTP_PASS=""
SMTP_FROM=""
